      "id": "adfbf06c-ee5f-48c7-a053-49802d533448",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great example to learn **nested classes vs inner classes** 👍\nI’ll walk you through this **step-by-step**, explaining **what each class is**, **why it exists**, and **how `Main` uses it**.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Big Picture: What this code is demonstrating\n\nThis project shows **two different kinds of nested classes in Java**:\n\n| Type                         | Where                           | Example                         |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| **Static Nested Class**      | Inside a class, marked `static` | `Employee.EmployeeComparator`   |\n| **Inner Class (Non-static)** | Inside a class, *not* static    | `StoreEmployee.StoreComparator` |\n\nKey difference:\n\n* **Static nested class** → does **NOT** need an object of outer class\n* **Inner class** → **REQUIRES** an object of the outer class\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ `Employee` class (Base class)\n\n### Fields\n\n```java\nprivate int employeeId;\nprivate String name;\nprivate int yearStarted;\n```\n\nRepresents a generic employee.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Static Nested Class: `EmployeeComparator`\n\n```java\npublic static class EmployeeComparator<T extends Employee>\n        implements Comparator<Employee> {\n```\n\n#### Why `static`?\n\n* It **does not depend on any specific Employee object**\n* It only compares two employees passed to it\n\n#### How sorting works\n\n```java\nif (sortType.equalsIgnoreCase(\"yearStarted\")) {\n    return o1.yearStarted - o2.yearStarted;\n}\nreturn o1.name.compareTo(o2.name);\n```\n\nSo this comparator can:\n\n* Sort by **name** (default)\n* Sort by **yearStarted** (if specified)\n\n---\n\n### Usage in `Main`\n\n```java\nemployees.sort(\n    new Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\").reversed()\n);\n```\n\n✅ Key points:\n\n* `Employee.EmployeeComparator` → static nested class\n* `.reversed()` → reverses the sorting order\n* No `Employee` object needed to create it\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Why is `EmployeeComparator.java` still present?\n\n```java\npublic class EmployeeComparator<T extends Employee>\n        implements Comparator<Employee> {\n```\n\nThis is a **top-level comparator class**, used earlier in the course.\n\n### Why it’s commented out in `Main`\n\n```java\n// var comparator = new EmployeeComparator<>();\n// employees.sort(comparator);\n```\n\nBecause now you’re learning:\n\n> **How nested classes can replace external utility classes**\n\n📌 Nested class keeps comparison logic **closer to the data it operates on**\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ `StoreEmployee` class (Subclass)\n\n```java\npublic class StoreEmployee extends Employee {\n    private String store;\n}\n```\n\nAdds store-specific data like:\n\n* Target\n* Walmart\n* Macys\n\nOverrides `toString()`:\n\n```java\nreturn \"%-8s%s\".formatted(store, super.toString());\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Inner Class: `StoreComparator`\n\n```java\npublic class StoreComparator<T extends StoreEmployee>\n        implements Comparator<StoreEmployee> {\n```\n\n🚨 This is **NOT static**\n\n### Why inner (non-static)?\n\nBecause:\n\n* It directly accesses `store`\n* It belongs to **a specific StoreEmployee instance**\n\n---\n\n### Comparison logic\n\n```java\nint result = o1.store.compareTo(o2.store);\nif (result == 0) {\n    return new Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\")\n            .compare(o1, o2);\n}\nreturn result;\n```\n\nSorting rules:\n\n1. **First by store name**\n2. If store names are equal → **sort by yearStarted**\n\nThis shows:\n\n* Inner class using a **static nested class**\n* Clean reuse of logic\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Creating an Inner Class (IMPORTANT)\n\n```java\nvar comparator = new StoreEmployee().new StoreComparator<>();\n```\n\nWhy this syntax?\n\nBecause:\n\n* `StoreComparator` is an **inner class**\n* Inner classes **require an outer class instance**\n\nGeneral rule:\n\n```java\nOuter outer = new Outer();\nOuter.Inner inner = outer.new Inner();\n```\n\nThat’s exactly what’s happening here.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Why this design is GOOD\n\n### ✅ Encapsulation\n\n* Sorting logic lives **inside the class it belongs to**\n\n### ✅ Readability\n\n* `Employee.EmployeeComparator`\n* `StoreEmployee.StoreComparator`\n\nYou instantly know **what they sort**\n\n### ✅ Real-world pattern\n\nThis is commonly used in:\n\n* Domain models\n* Comparator logic\n* Strategy-style implementations\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Final Flow in `Main`\n\n### Employees\n\n1. Create list\n2. Sort by `yearStarted` (descending)\n3. Print\n\n### StoreEmployees\n\n1. Create list\n2. Sort by:\n\n   * Store name\n   * Then yearStarted\n3. Print\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ One-line takeaway (important for interviews)\n\n> **Static nested classes don’t need an outer instance; inner classes do — and inner classes can access outer class data directly.**\n\n---\n\nIf you want next:\n\n* 🔁 Convert inner class → lambda\n* 🧠 When to use nested vs top-level class\n* 🧪 Debug this step-by-step with memory diagrams\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
